ncies-first order.\n\nWait, if I follow forward edges from A1: A1 → [B1, C1] where B1 and C1 depend on A1. And B1 → [D1]. So dependents of A1 = {B1, C1, D1}.\n\nTopological order: A1, [B1, C1], D1 — but A1 already has its new value. I just need to recompute B1, C1, D1 in order such that if B1 depends on C1 (or vice versa), they're in the right order.\n\nSince I follow forward edges, the natural order from a DFS/BFS may or may not be topological. Let me use Kahn's algorithm or a simple DFS-based topological sort.\n\nActually, for this specific use case, I think I can get away with a simpler approach:\n- Starting from the changed cell, DFS through dependents\n- Compute topological order using post-order traversal\n- Recompute in that order\n\nOr even simpler: since the graph is a DAG (we check for cycles), I can:\n1. Do a DFS from the changed cell following forward edges\n2. Record the order in which cells are first visited (pre-order)\n3. Reverse it\n\nHmm, that's not guaranteed to give a topological order. Let me use the standard DFS approach:\n\n```\nfunction topologicalSort(start: string): string[] {\n  const visited = new Set<string>();\n  const order: string[] = [];\n  \n  function dfs(cell: string) {\n    if (visited.has(cell)) return;\n    visited.add(cell);\n    for (const dep of dependents.get(cell) ?? []) {\n      dfs(dep);\n    }\n    order.push(cell); // post-order\n  }\n  \n  dfs(start);\n  return order; // reversed: dependents first, then cells that depend on them\n}\n```\n\nPost-order means: first visit all dependents, then add current. So dependents come later. But I need to recompute a cell's precedents first (the cells it depends on).\n\nWait, let me think about direction again:\n- Edge: A1 → B1 (B1 depends on A1)\n- When A1 changes, I need to recompute B1\n- If B1 → C1 (C1 depends on B1), I need to recompute C1 too\n- Order: recompute B1 first (since it depends directly on A1), then C1 (since it depends on B1)\n\nSo if I follow forward edges from A