rations. For functions like COUNT, I'm already checking if values are numeric.\n\nOK let me start coding. Let me create all the files.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the module system. The project uses \"module\": \"Node16\" so imports need `.js` extensions in TypeScript. I need to use `import { ... } from './foo.js'` even for importing .ts files.\n\nActually, with Node16 module resolution, TypeScript expects `.js` extensions in imports. When compiling to JS, it will produce `./foo.js` files. And since we're using `tsx` for testing, it handles the `.js` extensions in imports from `.ts` files.\n\nOK let me start writing the code.\n\nLet me create the files one by one:\n\n1. `src/reference.ts` - Cell reference utilities\n2. `src/tokenizer.ts` - Tokenizer\n3. `src/parser.ts` - Parser\n4. `src/evaluator.ts` - Evaluator\n5. `src/dependencyGraph.ts` - Dependency graph\n6. `src/spreadsheet.ts` - Spreadsheet class\n7. `src/index.ts` - Exports\n8. `src/spreadsheet.test.ts` - Tests\n\nLet me write all of them now.\n\nLet me plan the evaluation more carefully.\n\nWhen a cell contains a formula like `=SUM(A1:A5)`, I need to:\n1. Tokenize `SUM(A1:A5)` (formula body without the leading `=`)\n2. Parse into AST\n3. Evaluate AST to get the value\n\nFor the evaluator, I need:\n- A way to look up cell values\n- A way to resolve ranges into individual cell references\n\nThe evaluator function takes an AST node and returns a value. It needs access to the spreadsheet's cell values.\n\nLet me think about how to structure the evaluator. I could make it a method of the Spreadsheet class, or a separate function that takes a context object.\n\nI'll make it a separate function that takes a \"resolver\" (function to get cell values).\n\nAlright, let me write the code now.\n\nActually, one more thing: how to handle `=` as comparison operator vs. formula prefix.\n\nIn `=A1=5`, the first `=` is the formula prefix. The second `=` is the comparison operator.\nIn `=A1`, there's no comparison, it's just a reference.\n\nSo when parsing the formula body (after stripping the leading `=`), I treat `=` as the equality comparison operator.\n\nFor the tokenizer, when I see `=`:\n- If it's the first character of the formula body, could be confusing. But actually, after stripping the `=`, if the formula starts with `=`, that would mean... no wait, I strip the leading `=` from the formula prefix. So the body `A1=5` contains `=` as comparison operator.\n\nActually, there's a subtlety: `A1=B1` means \"A1 equals B1\" (comparison). But `=A1=B1` is the formula with prefix stripped to get `A1=B1`. So in the body `A1=B1`, the `=` is a comparison operator.\n\nLet me also think about how to handle `<>` (not equals). In the tokenizer, I need to handle two-character operators.\n\nToken types:\n```\nNUMBER: \\d+(\\.\\d+)?\nSTRING: \"[^\"]*\"\nBOOLEAN: true|false\nCELL_REF: [A-Z]+[1-9]\\d*\nFUNCTION: [A-Z_]+ (followed by '(')\nOP: +, -, *, /\nCOMP: >, <, >=, <=, =, <>\nLPAREN: (\nRPAREN: )\nCOMMA: ,\nCOLON: :\n```\n\nFor the function token, I need to distinguish between a function name and a plain identifier. Since all functions are followed by `(`, I can look ahead one token. Or I can tokenize all [A-Z]+ as identifiers and let the parser decide.\n\nActually, function names like SUM, AVERAGE, etc. are all uppercase. Cell refs are also uppercase but contain digits. So the tokenizer can distinguish:\n- `SUM` → FUNCTION (followed by `(`)\n- `A1` → CELL_REF (has digits)\n- `A` → Hmm, is this a function or a cell ref? Single letter without digits could be either. But in practice, `A` alone would be parsed in context. If followed by `(`, it's a function, otherwise it's a single-letter cell ref... but cells have row numbers. So `A` alone is a column reference without a row number, which doesn't make sense as a cell ref.\n\nWait, the spec says cell references are like `A1`, `B2`, `Z100` - column letters followed by row number. So I need: one or more letters followed by one or more digits. Tha